            <div xml:id="d738">

                <pb facs="0801" n="792" sameAs="frus:frus1938v05/pg792"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.1731/334: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Long</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-02-16T19:00:00-05:00">February 16, 1938—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>14. Your despatch No. 832, February 9.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d738-f1"/> Please
                    confirm immediately by telegram Department’s understanding that the statement in
                        <pb facs="0802" n="793" xml:id="pg793"/> the third paragraph of your note
                    No. 235 handed on February 8 to the Foreign Minister regarding no increase being
                    contemplated by Nicaragua in “basic rates of duty now specified in Schedule I of
                    the trade agreement”, means the rates set forth in Schedule I rather than,
                    wherever different, the rates of the Nicaraguan tariff in effect prior to the
                    effective date of the agreement. For example, on hog lard (Nicaraguan tariff
                    item 956) the Department understands that the rate in effect after March 10 will
                    remain 10 centavos per net kilo for American lard and not return to 12 centavos,
                    the rate applicable prior to the agreement.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
